1
00:00:01,083 --> 00:00:02,563
That sensor can't see him.

2
00:00:02,643 --> 00:00:04,888
It's pointed at a plain, blank wall.

3
00:00:04,968 --> 00:00:07,768
Yet researchers have
used light bouncing off

4
00:00:07,848 --> 00:00:10,288
a wall to track someone
hidden around a corner.

5
00:00:10,368 --> 00:00:14,095
That dot is their position
estimate. Not a photograph.

6
00:00:14,406 --> 00:00:18,433
The trick is timing: light that reaches
him takes the long way round,

7
00:00:18,513 --> 00:00:19,853
so it comes back later.

8
00:00:19,933 --> 00:00:22,003
A few billionths of a second later.

9
00:00:22,083 --> 00:00:24,003
This takes a time-of-flight sensor,

10
00:00:24,083 --> 00:00:26,648
a camera that clocks
its own light's round trip.

11
00:00:26,728 --> 00:00:30,535
So how do you turn a tiny
delay into a location?

12
00:00:30,678 --> 00:00:34,271
First, a puzzle: why does
a plain wall work at all?

13
00:00:34,351 --> 00:00:37,428
Put a mirror here,
and our friend is simply visible.

14
00:00:37,508 --> 00:00:39,988
A painted wall is rough up close.

15
00:00:40,068 --> 00:00:44,228
It throws light every which way,
so each spot acts less like a mirror,

16
00:00:44,308 --> 00:00:45,868
more like a tiny lamp.

17
00:00:45,948 --> 00:00:48,885
Everything coming back
is a blend of many paths.

18
00:00:48,965 --> 00:00:50,988
What survives is timing.

19
00:00:51,068 --> 00:00:54,453
Nearly everything coming back
bounced once, off the wall.

20
00:00:54,533 --> 00:00:57,583
Our friend's echo bounced
three times, so it's tiny.

21
00:00:57,663 --> 00:00:59,863
This is real data from the same team,

22
00:00:59,943 --> 00:01:04,623
with a different sensor and hidden
object: the wall's big echo, then,

23
00:01:04,703 --> 00:01:08,436
a few nanoseconds later,
a bump you have to zoom in to see.

24
00:01:08,516 --> 00:01:11,503
In this capture, hundreds of times weaker.

25
00:01:11,583 --> 00:01:13,233
That bump is the clue.

26
00:01:13,313 --> 00:01:16,403
Its timing says how much
farther the light travelled.

27
00:01:16,483 --> 00:01:17,843
Farther from where?

28
00:01:17,923 --> 00:01:22,088
Simplify it: the sensor flashes
and listens at one spot on the wall.

29
00:01:22,168 --> 00:01:25,448
Light covers about thirty
centimetres every nanosecond.

30
00:01:25,528 --> 00:01:29,928
But the detour is there and back,
so each extra nanosecond puts him

31
00:01:30,008 --> 00:01:33,163
only about fifteen centimetres
farther from the wall spot.

32
00:01:33,243 --> 00:01:36,838
Measure the extra delay, and you
know how far he is from that spot.

33
00:01:36,918 --> 00:01:39,253
Not which direction. Just how far.

34
00:01:39,333 --> 00:01:43,223
He could be anywhere on this arc,
all the same distance from that spot.

35
00:01:43,303 --> 00:01:45,143
Now listen at a second spot.

36
00:01:45,223 --> 00:01:47,328
Another delay, another arc.

37
00:01:47,408 --> 00:01:51,268
On this side of the wall,
they cross in just one place.

38
00:01:51,348 --> 00:01:55,228
Measured timings are a bit fuzzy,
so each arc is really a band,

39
00:01:55,308 --> 00:01:57,433
and the bands overlap in a small patch.

40
00:01:57,513 --> 00:02:01,033
These two spots are close,
so the patch is long and blurry.

41
00:02:01,113 --> 00:02:02,853
Spread them out, and it shrinks.

42
00:02:02,933 --> 00:02:06,293
In practice, a computer
weighs many spots at once,

43
00:02:06,373 --> 00:02:09,743
trying positions and keeping those
whose predicted echoes match.

44
00:02:09,823 --> 00:02:13,563
It also leans on assumptions,
like what kind of object it's after.

45
00:02:13,643 --> 00:02:17,496
That's why the answer is a likely
location, or a rough shape.

46
00:02:17,576 --> 00:02:19,483
And here's a real one.

47
00:02:19,563 --> 00:02:23,083
Moved through known positions,
the sensor behind that faint bump

48
00:02:23,163 --> 00:02:25,930
rebuilt the rough outline of a hidden U.

49
00:02:28,953 --> 00:02:34,073
In 2012, an MIT team rebuilt the rough
3D shape of a hidden mannequin.

50
00:02:34,153 --> 00:02:37,668
By 2021, others had live
video around corners.

51
00:02:37,748 --> 00:02:39,828
But those ran on research equipment.

52
00:02:39,908 --> 00:02:43,093
One team had even tracked hidden
objects with a cheap sensor.

53
00:02:43,173 --> 00:02:46,133
Then, in a study published in 2026,

54
00:02:46,213 --> 00:02:50,293
a team from MIT and Dartmouth tried
the small time-of-flight sensors,

55
00:02:50,373 --> 00:02:53,548
often called LiDAR,
found in phones and gadgets.

56
00:02:53,628 --> 00:02:55,708
Don't expect your phone to do this yet:

57
00:02:55,788 --> 00:02:58,398
phone makers often keep
the raw data private.

58
00:02:58,478 --> 00:03:02,198
Their new idea: a model
that keeps track of what moved,

59
00:03:02,278 --> 00:03:04,763
so many weak frames can be combined.

60
00:03:04,843 --> 00:03:07,503
Why would a smaller,
cheaper sensor need that?

61
00:03:07,583 --> 00:03:09,863
Weak lasers mean fainter echoes.

62
00:03:09,943 --> 00:03:13,143
The team's smartphone-grade
device had about a hundred pixels:

63
00:03:13,223 --> 00:03:14,903
a hundred listening spots.

64
00:03:14,983 --> 00:03:17,328
And if you hold one in your
hand, it jiggles.

65
00:03:17,408 --> 00:03:20,608
Their fix borrows night mode's
trick from phone cameras:

66
00:03:20,688 --> 00:03:24,340
stack many quick, dim frames
into one better estimate.

67
00:03:24,420 --> 00:03:27,233
The catch: things move between frames.

68
00:03:27,313 --> 00:03:30,080
He steps, and his echo shifts in time.

69
00:03:30,193 --> 00:03:33,778
The sensor jiggles, and its listening
spots land somewhere new.

70
00:03:33,858 --> 00:03:38,318
Just add them up, and echoes
from different places blur into one smear,

71
00:03:38,398 --> 00:03:40,678
like a long exposure of someone walking.

72
00:03:40,758 --> 00:03:43,508
So their model solves
for one unknown at a time.

73
00:03:43,588 --> 00:03:44,668
To build a shape,

74
00:03:44,748 --> 00:03:48,228
hold the object still and step
the sensor through known positions,

75
00:03:48,308 --> 00:03:51,388
spreading out its listening spots.
That's how the U was made.

76
00:03:51,468 --> 00:03:53,908
To follow a person, hold the sensor still,

77
00:03:53,988 --> 00:03:56,748
and let each guess drift
a little between frames,

78
00:03:56,828 --> 00:03:58,668
keeping the ones that still match.

79
00:03:58,748 --> 00:04:01,675
The estimate keeps up instead of smearing.

80
00:04:02,248 --> 00:04:05,668
So what can it actually do,
and where does it fail?

81
00:04:05,748 --> 00:04:07,148
Take our opening clip:

82
00:04:07,228 --> 00:04:10,908
an off-the-shelf kit the authors
put at under a hundred dollars,

83
00:04:10,988 --> 00:04:13,753
held still while a person
walked behind a partition.

84
00:04:13,833 --> 00:04:15,553
Many of their tests had help:

85
00:04:15,633 --> 00:04:18,433
safety-vest style reflective
material on the target,

86
00:04:18,513 --> 00:04:20,593
which sends far more light straight back.

87
00:04:20,673 --> 00:04:24,148
Our clip's files don't say
if the walker wore any.

88
00:04:24,228 --> 00:04:25,668
But in a separate test,

89
00:04:25,748 --> 00:04:28,708
the authors report tracking
a person in ordinary clothes,

90
00:04:28,788 --> 00:04:30,973
capturing thirty frames a second.

91
00:04:31,053 --> 00:04:32,813
What might this be good for?

92
00:04:32,893 --> 00:04:36,093
Picture a delivery robot nearing
a blind warehouse corner.

93
00:04:36,173 --> 00:04:38,173
With a suitable wall at the junction,

94
00:04:38,253 --> 00:04:42,893
a sensor like this might give it
an early hint of movement out of sight.

95
00:04:42,973 --> 00:04:46,413
Just a fuzzy blob:
enough to say slow down,

96
00:04:46,493 --> 00:04:48,033
not enough to say who's there.

97
00:04:48,113 --> 00:04:52,993
And plenty is still hard:
short range, dark or shiny walls,

98
00:04:53,073 --> 00:04:56,358
bright sunlight, and fast
math on small hardware.

99
00:04:56,438 --> 00:04:59,293
The researchers call it
an early-stage prototype.

100
00:04:59,373 --> 00:05:03,578
For now, it's a promising clue,
not a safety system.

101
00:05:03,658 --> 00:05:07,648
Being out of sight isn't
the same as giving nothing away.

102
00:05:07,728 --> 00:05:11,055
To really hide, he'd have
to block the bounces too.

103
00:05:13,763 --> 00:05:18,163
This is Future Got Weird:
the strange future, explained.

104
00:05:18,243 --> 00:05:21,537
Subscribe for more, and we'll
see you around the corner.
